  "subject": "💡 Axios Finish Line: Your life lessons",
  "date": "Wed, 14 Oct 2026 21:29:50 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🎨 Plus: Reclining masterpiece | Wednesday, October 14, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 14, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n 🐪 Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 636 words … 2½ mins. Edited by Natalie Daher and copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: The family stories we carry \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n The stories we carry often begin with small interactions or offhand comments that evolve into advice or lore repeated for decades.\n\n After we asked readers to share the family stories and wisdom that have stayed with them, you all delivered, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. Here are some of those responses, edited for brevity: \n\n 📖 Stories that outlive us: Bob Schumacher of Wheaton, Ill., is compiling \"Aunt Margaret stories\" — to honor his late sister-in-law, veterinarian Margaret Knoll, who died in 2012. \n\n 🐕 Her outrageous work stories  range from constipated snakes to dogs eating household furnishings. The tales live on, even though she never had kids: Bob's grandchildren began requesting \"Aunt Margaret stories\" instead of fairy tales. So the family put together 75 pages of her adventures, bringing joy and comfort to Margaret's mother, now in her 90s. Bob writes: \"Her dying fear was that she would be forgotten.\" Now her stories are preserved for generations. 🐎 A family legend: Over a beer in Wisconsin, Karl Held's grandfather shared the story of an ancestor who supposedly worked as an equestrian master in Prussia before emigrating to America.\n\n According to family lore, a wealthy man hired him to manage his stables in Alsace. He later emigrated to America with the man's daughter. But there was another version: \"That or some say he was a horse thief who ran off with the girl and some horses and fled to America to escape from her father,\" his grandfather recalled. 🫶 Ask for help: Trish Coughlin, of Atlanta, remembers a piece of advice from her mother-in-law, who had raised two boys as a single mom.\n\n \"I really regret not asking for or hiring more help,\" her mother-in-law told her as Trish was raising three sons. Hearing that from someone she admired relieved her own guilt about asking for help. She has since passed on the lesson to her daughter-in-law: \"Her gift is helping future generations!\" 🎖️ A story that changed a son's view of his father: Rob Malinski, of Austin, Texas, worked at the same postal facility as his dad, and the two regularly drove to work together.\n\n One day Rob was reading a book about World War II naval history when his father casually mentioned: \"Oh, I saw that.\" His father, Albert, eventually revealed that he had served on Leyte Island in the Philippines, and told Rob he had witnessed the Battle of Surigao Strait . Weeks of conversations followed, sometimes through tears. Rob writes: \"Eventually my dad recovered from his alcoholism, and I forgave him for some of the sad parts of my childhood. I now understand he was part of the greatest generation who fought in WWII.\" 🎬 Imagine the future: Holly Cascio's grandmother, Elizabeth Redling, was born in Brooklyn in 1921 and lived to 101.\n\n As a child, she once made a frigid walk to the cinema with her mother and wondered what it would be like to watch a movie at home. \" She was amazed when it actually became reality,\" Holly, of Long Island, N.Y., writes. Holly's lesson: Modern conveniences were once unimaginable — and \"it's okay to be bored and daydream once in a while.\" 👖 Everyone's human: Lori Ramus, of Columbiana, Ohio, says her father, John, was a great listener who offered simple advice when she felt intimidated by someone.\n\n \"Everyone puts their pants on the same way in the morning.\" Lori says the line has helped her \"get along with people in all walks of life!\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Find where your team is misaligned \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Simplify Workshop shows your team where it is misaligned and why it keeps happening.\n\n Everyone leaves with one aligned set of priorities and a real kill list, built on the Confront, Delete, Amplify method. The whole team works through it together.\n\n Learn more about the Workshop \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🦩 Parting shot: Florida in full color \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Nancy Broen A spectacular Florida sunset graces the sky in Babcock Ranch, America's first solar-powered town, in Southwest Florida.\n\n 🚲 Reader Frank Broen tells us his wife, Nancy, snapped this shot while the couple rode their recumbent tandem trike through the community. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
